{"name":"Brewed Mustard","hex":"#e68364","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"brewed","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of brew"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/brewed"},{"term":"mustard","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant of certain species of the genus Brassica, or of related genera (especially Sinapis alba, in the family Brassicaceae, with yellow flowers, and linear seed pods).","Powder or paste made from seeds of the mustard plant, and used as a condiment or a spice."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a dark yellow colour.","Very good, or very skilled."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mustard"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mustard (album)","blocks":[{"text":"Mustard is the second solo album by English musician Roy Wood, released in December 1975 by Jet Records. The album was recorded at De Lane Lea Studios and Phonogram Studios, although a dispute at one of the studios delayed the release of the album. Produced and entirely performed by Wood, who also designed the album artwork, Mustard was a departure from his previous solo album Boulders (1973), with a more ambitious approach and denser, more layered production, again mixing a number of musical styles. Annie Haslam and Phil Everly contributed guest vocals to the album; Wood's influences on the record included the Andrews Sisters, the Beach Boys and Led Zeppelin."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/e/ea/Roy_Wood_Mustard.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mustard_(album)"},{"title":"Brewing","blocks":[{"text":"Brewing is the production of beer by steeping a starch source in water and fermenting the resulting sweet liquid with yeast. It may be done in a brewery by a commercial brewer, at home by a homebrewer, or communally. Brewing has taken place since at least the 11th millennium BC, and archaeological evidence suggests that prehistoric peoples and later civilisations, including those in ancient Egypt, China, and Mesopotamia, brewed beer. Since the nineteenth century the brewing industry has been part of most western economies."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/The_Brewer_designed_and_engraved_in_the_Sixteenth._Century_by_J_Amman.png/330px-The_Brewer_designed_and_engraved_in_the_Sixteenth._Century_by_J_Amman.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brewing"},{"title":"Mustard (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Mustard is a dull/dark yellow color that resembles culinary mustard. It is similar to the color Flax."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mustard_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/DeLaneLea.JPG/500px-DeLaneLea.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/DeLaneLea.JPG/1280px-DeLaneLea.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/DeLaneLea.JPG/500px-DeLaneLea.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"De Lane Lea Studios in London, where half of Mustard was recorded.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADeLaneLea.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/The_Andrews_Sisters_1952.JPG/500px-The_Andrews_Sisters_1952.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/The_Andrews_Sisters_1952.JPG/500px-The_Andrews_Sisters_1952.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Andrews_Sisters_1952.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/John_Bonham_1975.jpg/500px-John_Bonham_1975.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/John_Bonham_1975.jpg/500px-John_Bonham_1975.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJohn_Bonham_1975.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Colman_mustard_1499.JPG/500px-Colman_mustard_1499.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Colman_mustard_1499.JPG/1280px-Colman_mustard_1499.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Colman_mustard_1499.JPG/500px-Colman_mustard_1499.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"To promote Mustard, Jet Records co-operated with Colman's by giving away jars of the brand's mustard in competitions.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AColman_mustard_1499.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Alulu_Beer_Receipt.jpg/250px-Alulu_Beer_Receipt.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f7/Alulu_Beer_Receipt.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Alulu_Beer_Receipt.jpg/250px-Alulu_Beer_Receipt.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Alulu beer receipt records a purchase of \"best\" beer from a brewer, c. 2050 BC, from the Sumerian city of Umma in Mesopotamia (ancient Iraq).[11]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAlulu_Beer_Receipt.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Sjb_whiskey_